This looks like early 2015. Unemployment is 5.7% (January 2015 was 5.7%), headline CPI is -0.19% YoY (January 2015 was -0.1%), and the dollar is up 11.6% YoY. Fed funds at 0.11 and a 10-year yield of 2.03 also fit.

Month: February 2015 had unemployment at 5.5%, so January 2015 at 5.7% fits better, though the data may be as of end of February. Payrolls averaged 336k over 3 months (Nov 423, Dec 329, Jan 257 gives 336), which matches January 2015 data. The 10-year yield of 2.03% at end of January was about 1.68%, and at end of February was about 2.0%. The 1-month equity return of +5.3% and the 20y+ Treasury return of -5.1% match February 2015, when yields rose. Gold fell 5.9% in February. So the snapshot is at the end of February 2015, with the latest published unemployment rate for January (5.7%) as well as the payroll data through January.

